![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

The `git -C` command expects a directory path and will fail with a fatal error if passed a file path (such as `realFilePath` for a `--body-file`). This causes `_gitCommonDir` to return an empty string, which breaks the `_sameGitRepository` check for all files outside of `/tmp` or the current working directory.

Note that the test `allows absolute --body-file from a linked worktree for the same repo` passed only because the test fixture was created inside `tmpdir()`, which is always allowed by `_resolveAllowedBodyFilePath` regardless of the git repository check.

To fix this, we should resolve the directory of the path using `dirname` if it is a file before running the `git` command.

```suggestion
function _gitCommonDir(path) {
  let dir = path;
  try {
    if (existsSync(path) && !statSync(path).isDirectory()) {
      dir = dirname(path);
    }
  } catch {}
  const topLevel = _gitValue(["-C", dir, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"]);
  if (!topLevel) return "";
  const commonDir = _gitValue(["-C", topLevel, "rev-parse", "--git-common-dir"]);
  if (!commonDir) return "";
  return _safeRealpath(isAbsolute(commonDir) ? commonDir : resolve(topLevel, commonDir));
}
```

