![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

The `git -C <path>` command expects a directory. If `<path>` is a file (which `realFilePath` always is, since it represents the `--body-file`), `git -C` will fail with `fatal: cannot change to '...': Not a directory`. This causes `gitCommonDir` to always return `""` for files, breaking the `sameGitRepository` check.

We should resolve the directory of the path using `dirname` if it is a file before passing it to `git -C`.

```javascript
function gitCommonDir(path) {
  if (!path) return "";
  const dir = existsSync(path) && statSync(path).isDirectory() ? path : dirname(path);
  const topLevel = gitValue(["-C", dir, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"]);
  if (!topLevel) return "";
  const commonDir = gitValue(["-C", topLevel, "rev-parse", "--git-common-dir"]);
  if (!commonDir) return "";
  return safeRealpath(isAbsolute(commonDir) ? commonDir : resolve(topLevel, commonDir));
}
```

![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

Import `statSync` from `fs` and `dirname` from `path` to support checking if a path is a directory and extracting its directory name in `gitCommonDir`.

```suggestion
import { existsSync, readFileSync, appendFileSync, realpathSync, statSync } from "fs";
import { execFileSync } from "child_process";
import { isAbsolute, resolve, sep, dirname } from "path";
```

